{
 "id": "M26",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "Most hard backdoors stay flat across all three monitors",
 "section": "results",
 "order": 137,
 "caption": "A variation on M22. One line per hard backdoor, 51 of them, tracking the same code across the three monitors that read it. A line that climbs on the right is a backdoor Opus found suspicious and the small monitors did not. 30 of the lines never leave the bottom band.",
 "notes": [
  "Same data as M22, read along rather than down. This form answers whether a backdoor's score is a property of the program or of the monitor: a bundle of parallel lines would mean the program, and a fan means the monitor.",
  "Violet lines are backdoors no monitor caught. Grey lines are the ones a panel monitor did catch, which is why they are in this hard set but not in the never-caught group of 41.",
  "The three columns are not a scale or a sequence. They are three monitors in a fixed order, so the horizontal distance between them means nothing and only the vertical moves do.",
  "Sonnet and Haiku are nearly flat against each other, medians 1 and 2 out of 100, which is what makes this set hard. Almost all of the movement is in the last step.",
  "Lines overlap heavily at the bottom, so the band below 20 is a thicket rather than 30 readable lines. Use M22 to read an individual backdoor."
 ],
 "height": 560,
